From c6b075f24b4d526f8b10edb389a38279eb6ecd37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 21:50:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/54] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon memory objects, VA heap and
 bind/unbind
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Milestone items 3, 4 and 5. The driver can now hold a buffer: allocate
memory, reserve GPU address space and map one into the other.

Memory is NvMap-backed through horizon_gpu_mem. map() has nothing to do
— the storage behind an NvMap object is ordinary process memory that was
registered with the service, so it has a CPU address for its whole
lifetime and every map hands back the same pointer; nvkmd core does the
reference counting. sync_to_gpu and sync_from_gpu are the cache
maintenance homebrew heap memory needs, and horizon_gpu decides whether
it is required from the policy recorded at creation rather than from the
call site. log_handle is the in-process NvMap handle: the closest thing
this platform has to the GEM handle the field was named after, and a
real identifier rather than a number invented to fill it.

NVKMD_MEM_COHERENT is an uncached allocation (decision D14): NVK means
an uncached map by HOST_COHERENT on an SoC (nvk_physical_device.c), and
nvkmd_mem_sync_to_gpu / sync_from_gpu return before doing anything when
the flag is set, so a cached allocation behind it would get no
maintenance from anywhere. Everything else stays CACHED, which is what
heap memory is and what the explicit flush/invalidate around it expects.

Every memory object gets its own bound VA, because NVK reads
mem->va->addr directly and not as a special case — nvk_buffer.c,
nvk_device.c's zero page while vkCreateDevice is still running, and
nvk_cmd_buffer.c for every push. The nouveau backend establishes that
invariant inside its own alloc_mem and nothing in nvkmd core
re-establishes it for a backend that does not. free() releases the VA
before destroying the memory: releasing the reservation unmaps what is
bound in it, and horizon_gpu_mem_destroy refuses with ERR_BUSY while any
mapping is alive, so the other order would fail every free.

Two requests are refused by name instead of quietly downgraded:
NVKMD_MEM_VRAM (GM20B has none) and NVKMD_MEM_SHARED (no dma-buf, and
nvkmd_info already says so). NVKMD_MEM_MAP_FIXED likewise: NvMap chooses
the CPU address, and honouring the call at a different one would be
worse than failing.

VA reservation defaults to the small-page half of the address space,
which imposes the least on the binds inside it: horizon_gpu_vm_map uses
the reservation's page size for every mapping, and a reservation is not
always bound as one piece — nvk_mem_arena binds 64 KiB chunks at 64 KiB
offsets into a VA that is megabytes wide. The one exception is a
non-pitch (block-linear) PTE kind, which goes to the big-page half: the
Maxwell MMU only fills block-linear kinds in big pages, and such a
mapping in the small-page half faults on first access rather than
failing to map. When a bind fails, the error names the VA offset, the
memory offset, the range, the PTE kind and the reservation's page size —
the number that decides whether the offsets were legal.

Unbind checks the result before freeing the binding: horizon_gpu_vm_unmap
deliberately leaves the mapping alive on failure so the caller can
retry, and the binding is the only pointer to it. On failure the binding
goes back on the list and the caller gets the error with the mapping
still tracked; freeing first stranded the VA range permanently.

Two VA flags fail by name for one reason each: SPARSE, because that is
decision D12 and NVK would otherwise advertise sparseBinding on this
chip; and REPLAY, which needs fixed addresses. ALLOC_FIXED is refused
here and implemented later in this series, together with the shader
window reservation that needs it.

nvkmd unbinds by (offset, range) and never hands back what bind_mem
returned, so the VA remembers what it mapped where. An exact match is
required: horizon_gpu maps and unmaps whole mappings, and unbinding half
of one would leave the address space and that list disagreeing.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 items 3-5
Why: nvkmd's memory and VA ops are libdrm/GEM-shaped; on Horizon a
  memory object is an NvMap handle and a GPU VA is an explicit
  reservation, so the backend has to establish itself every invariant
  (a bound VA per object, a page-size half per reservation) that a BO
  gives the nouveau backend for free.
Evidence: libnvk.a is 94 482 bytes with all five backend objects in the
  archive and 0 TLS relocations; scripts/build-mesa-nvk.sh builds it.
  On hardware, 2026-07-28: dev->images' 1 MiB contiguous VA landed in
  the big-page half, its first 0x10000 chunk's size rounded up to
  0x20000, no longer fit the memory object, and vkCreateDevice failed
  with VK_ERROR_UNKNOWN — the measurement behind the small-page default.
Upstream: no — this is the Horizon backend's own memory and VA path.

Absorbs (2026-08-23 series compaction; full history in mesa-patches/'s
own git log):

- 0023 "put block-linear mappings in the big-page half": the Maxwell MMU
  only fills block-linear kinds in big pages; choosing the half by size
  and alignment alone put some tiled images in the wrong one.
- 0028 "name the number that decides a failed bind": the bind failure
  message names the reservation's page size
  (horizon/include/horizon_gpu/vm.h alignment contract).
- 0030 "NVKMD_MEM_COHERENT is an uncached allocation": decision D14;
  nvkmd.c returns early from sync_to_gpu/sync_from_gpu on the flag.
- 0031 "every memory object needs its own VA": nvk_buffer.c:271,
  nvk_device.c:295 and nvk_cmd_buffer.c:195 dereference mem->va with no
  NULL check.
- 0034 "the reservation's size does not pick its page half": measured on
  hardware 2026-07-28 (the vkCreateDevice failure quoted above); also
  stopped logging a VA freed with memory still bound as a driver bug —
  it is nvk_mem_arena_finish's ordinary path.
- 0043 "keep the binding when unbind fails": found by review on PR #6.
